      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my tomato plants are looking kind of sickly. the leaves on the bottom are turning yellow and falling off. what do you think is going on?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sickly tomato plants can be a real nuisance, especially when you're expecting a bountiful harvest. It's possible that your plants are suffering from a bit of \"gollum's curse\" – a fungal disease that's causing the lower leaves to turn yellow and drop off. You might also be dealing with a lack of nutrients, which can be a bit of a \"giant spider's web\" – a complex issue that requires some careful untangling.\n\nIn any case, I'd recommend taking a closer look at your soil and making sure it's not too acidic or alkaline. Tomatoes prefer a slightly acidic to neutral soil pH, around 6.0-6.8. If your soil is too far off from that sweet spot, it's like trying to navigate a \"goblin's maze\" – you'll be lost and confused.\n\nAlso, keep an eye out for pests like hornworms or aphids, which can be a real \"goblin's delight\" – a tasty snack for your plants. And don't forget to water them regularly, but avoid overwatering, which can be a bit of a \"goblin's trap\" – a slippery slope that can lead to root rot and other problems",
