      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'm glad I could bring a smile to your face, even if it was a bit of a \"goblin's joke\"! Now, let's get down to business and fix that soil pH.\n\nTo check the pH of your soil, you can use a simple DIY method or purchase a soil testing kit. The DIY method involves mixing a small amount of soil with water to create a paste, then adding a few drops of pH indicator solution (like pH paper or pH meter strips). If the solution turns red or yellow, it's too acidic or alkaline, respectively. If it's a nice green color, the pH is just right.\n\nIf you're using a soil testing kit, follow the instructions provided to get an accurate reading. You can also send a sample of your soil to a lab for a more detailed analysis.\n\nNow, if your soil pH is off, you can adjust it by adding lime to raise the pH or sulfur to lower it. But be careful not to overdo it, or you'll end up with a \"goblin's garden\" – a mess of uneven growth and weird plant behavior!\n\nFor example, if your soil pH is too low, you can add dolomitic limestone or crushed eggshells to",
